WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.472
Callbacks and asynchronous work are lifetime-sensitive because they separate the time access is captured from the time access is used.

2
00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:13.521
A method may register a callback, queue work, start a thread, or capture this in a lambda.

3
00:00:13.521 --> 00:00:19.213
The original object may be destroyed before that work runs, leaving the callback with stale access.

4
00:00:19.213 --> 00:00:23.839
Capturing this is not automatically wrong, but it requires a visible lifetime guarantee.

5
00:00:23.839 --> 00:00:27.753
The owner might guarantee that the callback is unregistered before destruction.

6
00:00:27.753 --> 00:00:32.022
The queued work might own the object for the duration of execution.

7
00:00:32.022 --> 00:00:37.715
Or the callback might use weak observation and do nothing if the target no longer exists.

8
00:00:37.715 --> 00:00:41.273
The important point is that the policy must be explicit.

9
00:00:41.273 --> 00:00:45.187
Keeping an object alive is different from making it thread-safe.

10
00:00:45.187 --> 00:00:51.947
A std::shared_ptr can prevent destruction while work runs, but it does not protect mutable state from data races.

11
00:00:51.947 --> 00:00:56.929
Concurrent designs still need synchronization, immutability, thread confinement, message passing, or another clear strategy.

12
00:00:56.929 --> 00:01:01.554
Use-after-free prevention and thread-safety design have to be reviewed together.

13
00:01:01.554 --> 00:01:04.045
Delayed execution also changes where cleanup belongs.

14
00:01:04.045 --> 00:01:10.449
Destructors should not assume that merely dropping a handle stops every pending operation unless the API guarantees it.

15
00:01:10.449 --> 00:01:15.786
Joining threads, draining queues, unregistering callbacks, canceling timers, and waiting for completion are ownership decisions.

16
00:01:15.786 --> 00:01:20.056
Each one should have a clear owner and a tested shutdown sequence.

17
00:01:20.056 --> 00:01:22.191
Cancellation and shutdown paths deserve tests.

18
00:01:22.191 --> 00:01:26.105
What happens when work is pending and the owner is destroyed?

19
00:01:26.105 --> 00:01:28.595
Can a callback run after unregistration begins?

20
00:01:28.595 --> 00:01:32.865
Are stored raw pointers still valid when a background job wakes up?

21
00:01:32.865 --> 00:01:38.558
Safe async code defines ownership, cancellation, and synchronization rules before relying on callbacks to behave well.

22
00:01:38.558 --> 00:01:41.760
Treat shutdown as normal behavior, not an exceptional corner.
